"url":"/docs/energy#-2-the-iron-age-of-grid-storage","content":"**Keywords**"},"52":{"id":"/docs/energy-50","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-2-the-iron-age-of-grid-storage","content":"iron–air battery · long-duration storage · grid storage · LCOS · Form Energy · dunkelflaute"},"53":{"id":"/docs/energy-51","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-2-the-iron-age-of-grid-storage","content":"**Language**"},"54":{"id":"/docs/energy-52","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-2-the-iron-age-of-grid-storage","content":"English"},"55":{"id":"/docs/energy-53","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-2-the-iron-age-of-grid-storage","content":"**File**"},"56":{"id":"/docs/energy-54","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-2-the-iron-age-of-grid-storage","content":"`iron_air.pdf` (283 kB)"},"57":{"id":"/docs/energy-55","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-full-text-read-in-the-browser-1","content":"**Direct links:** ⬇ Download PDF"},"58":{"id":"/docs/energy-56","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-abstract-1","content":"> Iron–air batteries convert iron to rust and back — an electrochemistry\n> with a theoretical 1.28 V, 960 mAh/g, and ~~1,200 Wh/kg of iron: the\n> energy of a kilogram of lithium-ion cells for the price of a few cents\n> of metal. This paper builds the complete case: the cell chemistry and\n> its four engineering killers (round-trip efficiency, parasitic hydrogen\n> evolution, the bifunctional air electrode, CO₂ and water management);\n> a transparent model of a 100 MW / 100 h = 10 GWh plant (17,000 t of\n> iron at under 1% of system cost, $200–400M capital versus $2.9B for the\n> equivalent lithium-ion stack); an LCOS analysis (~~$32/MWh for 100-hour\n> firmness versus $58–78/MWh for 4-hour Li-ion); the world-scale numbers\n> (8 TW / $4 trillion market to 2040, 1.5–2.3 Gt CO₂/yr avoided); a\n> week-long dunkelflaute operating simulation; regional case studies\n> (Texas, California, Germany, Poland); manufacturing and supply-chain\n> economics; safety, standards, and end-of-life; and an honest risk\n> section with hard due-diligence data (36–50% round-trip efficiency,\n> 40–100 cycles/year). The conclusion: iron–air is the most credible\n> path to terawatt-scale multi-day storage — the engineering companion\n> to the firm baseload analyzed in the companion paper on superhot rock\n> geothermal."},"59":{"id":"/docs/energy-57","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"**Digging for the Sun: Superhot Rock Geothermal as the World-Scale\nSource of Clean Baseload Power**"},"60":{"id":"/docs/energy-58","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"*Bartosz Osiej — August 2026 · Review paper · CC-BY-4.0*"},"61":{"id":"/docs/energy-59","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"Property"},"62":{"id":"/docs/energy-60","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"Value"},"63":{"id":"/docs/energy-61","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"**Title**"},"64":{"id":"/docs/energy-62","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"Digging for the Sun: Superhot Rock Geothermal as the World-Scale Source of Clean Baseload Power"},"65":{"id":"/docs/energy-63","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"**Author**"},"66":{"id":"/docs/energy-64","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"Bartosz Osiej"},"67":{"id":"/docs/energy-65","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"**Published**"},"68":{"id":"/docs/energy-66","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"2026-08"},"69":{"id":"/docs/energy-67","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"**Type**"},"70":{"id":"/docs/energy-68","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"Technical review + quantitative techno-economic model"},"71":{"id":"/docs/energy-69","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"**License**"},"72":{"id":"/docs/energy-70","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"CC-BY-4.0"},"73":{"id":"/docs/energy-71","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"**Keywords**"},"74":{"id":"/docs/energy-72","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"superhot rock · supercritical geothermal · EGS · gyrotron drilling · firm power · baseload · LCOE"},"75":{"id":"/docs/energy-73","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"**Language**"},"76":{"id":"/docs/energy-74","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"English"},"77":{"id":"/docs/energy-75","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"**File**"},"78":{"id":"/docs/energy-76","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-3-digging-for-the-sun--superhot-rock-geothermal","content":"`superhot_geothermal.pdf` (277 kB)"},"79":{"id":"/docs/energy-77","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-full-text-read-in-the-browser-2","content":"**Direct links:** ⬇ Download PDF"},"80":{"id":"/docs/energy-78","page_id":"/docs/energy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/energy#-abstract-2","content":"> Wind and solar solved the cost problem; iron–air storage is solving\n> the multi-day problem; what remains is firm baseload for weeks-long\n> lulls, industrial heat, and energy security. Superhot rock (SHR)\n> geothermal extracts heat from rock at 374 °C and above — beyond the\n> supercritical point of water — reached by drilling 5–20 km. At those\n> conditions supercritical water carries 3–5× the enthalpy per kilogram,\n> so a single well delivers an order of magnitude more power than a\n> conventional well. The resource is effectively unlimited: 1% of the US\n> superhot resource is 4.3 TW. The honest bottleneck is drilling cost.\n> This paper reviews the physics (with real steam-table data), a century\n> of drilling history (Kola 12.3 km, Fenton Hill, Soultz, IDDP-1/2,\n> Fervo), the gyrotron drilling program, subsurface engineering,\n> regulation, and environmental footprint — and builds a transparent\n> 100 MWe techno-economic model. The honest central estimate is\n> $66–90/MWh at today's drilling costs (competitive with gas with carbon\n> cost); it falls to \\~$45/MWh only if the DOE Enhanced Geothermal Shot's\n> 90% drilling-cost reduction is delivered. The case in one sentence:\n> the geology is ready, the drilling is the bet, and the prize is\n> terawatt-scale anywhere-on-Earth clean baseload."},"81