on","content":"Two peers may see each other's presence at the same instant, and both must\nnot send offers (the WebRTC \"glare\" race). The peer with the **lexicographically\nsmaller id** sends the offer; the other side waits for it:"},"2253":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-34","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#2-glare-free-offer-selection","content":"Each peer id is random per session, so this always picks exactly one offerer."},"2254":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-35","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#3-signaling-messages-over-mqtt","content":"Signaling rides the same per-room topic as everything else. Message kinds:"},"2255":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-36","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#3-signaling-messages-over-mqtt","content":"The `to` field addresses a specific peer; others ignore it. ICE candidates\narriving before the remote description are buffered per connection."},"2256":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-37","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#4-relay-fallback-mqtt--why-it-exists","content":"Pure P2P in the browser **does not connect on mobile networks**: carriers\nuse CGNAT and reject hole-punching, and the free public TURN servers that\nused to bridge it are dead in 2026 or require an account (Cloudflare TURN\nneeds API credentials)."},"2257":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-38","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#4-relay-fallback-mqtt--why-it-exists","content":"The solution: every message is also **published in parallel** to the topic\n`n2mesh/{room}` on a public MQTT broker over WSS. The MQTT client is an\n**own, dependency-free MQTT 3.1.1 implementation** (\\~100 lines):"},"2258":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-39","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#4-relay-fallback-mqtt--why-it-exists","content":"**Room isolation:** every tab subscribes to the **exact topic of its room**\n(no wildcards) — traffic from other rooms never reaches it."},"2259":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-40","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#4-relay-fallback-mqtt--why-it-exists","content":"**Deduplication by ID:** every message has a unique `mid`; a `Map`\nremembers recent IDs for 30 s, so a message received over P2P *and* relay\n*and* the local bridge is displayed **once**."},"2260":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-41","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#4-relay-fallback-mqtt--why-it-exists","content":"**Offline queueing:** messages sent before the broker connection lands in\n`relay.queue` (with the room remembered) and are flushed after\nCONNACK→SUBACK."},"2261":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-42","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#4-relay-fallback-mqtt--why-it-exists","content":"**Brokers:** `wss://broker.hivemq.com:8884` (verified end-to-end), backup\n`wss://broker.emqx.io:8084`, failover every 3 attempts."},"2262":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-43","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#4-relay-fallback-mqtt--why-it-exists","content":"**Keepalive:** PINGREQ every 30 s, reconnect with backoff (1 s → 15 s)."},"2263":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-44","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#4-relay-fallback-mqtt--why-it-exists","content":"The status shows which path the connection uses: `P2P`, `P2P + relay`,\n`relay mode` (phone on CGNAT), `connecting…`."},"2264":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-45","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#5-local-bridge-broadcastchannel","content":"Two tabs of **the same browser** cannot connect via WebRTC (browsers block\nWebRTC loopback). The `BroadcastChannel` bridge connects them locally: tabs\nof the same browser exchange `hello`/`msg` directly, while different\nbrowsers/devices go through WebRTC data channels and/or the relay."},"2265":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-46","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#6-why-webtorrent-was-removed","content":"The original version found peers through public WebTorrent WebSocket trackers\n(`tracker.webtorrent.dev`, `tracker.openwebtorrent.com`) and carried messages\nover the BitTorrent extended protocol. Live testing (August 2026) showed the\ntrackers **accept announces and register peers but never relay WebRTC offers**\nbetween them — two clients in the same swarm (`complete=2`) received zero\noffers. The browser build of WebTorrent can only use WebSocket trackers (no\nUDP/DHT in the browser), so peers could never discover each other and P2P was\ndead. Replacing it with native WebRTC + MQTT signaling keeps the app fully\nserverless, zero-dependency, and working today."},"2266":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-47","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#files","content":"File"},"2267":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-48","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#files","content":"Contents"},"2268":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-49","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#files","content":"`index.html`"},"2269":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-50","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#files","content":"Single-page shell"},"2270":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-51","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#files","content":"`app.js`"},"2271":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-52","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#files","content":"Networking: WebRTC P2P + MQTT signaling/relay + local bridge"},"2272":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-53","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#files","content":"`style.css`"},"2273":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-54","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#files","content":"Dark aurora theme"},"2274":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-55","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#files","content":"`.github/workflows/deploy.yml`"},"2275":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-56","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#files","content":"Deploy to GitHub Pages"},"2276":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-57","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#limitations","content":"**No history** — when you leave the room, the peer group is gone (the price\nof having no server)."},"2277":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-58","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#limitations","content":"**Both peers must be online simultaneously** — the relay does not store\nmessages (QoS 0, no retention)."},"2278":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-59","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#limitations","content":"**Relay mode = messages pass through a public broker** — a deliberate\ntrade-off so the chat works on networks where P2P is impossible (CGNAT).\nP2P mode stays fully peer-to-peer."},"2279":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture-60","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh/architecture#repo","content":"BartoszOsiej/Meshcore ·\nLive"},"2280":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","type":"page","content":"💬 Meshcore — P2P Chat","breadcrumbs":["Catalog","📁 Projects","💬 Meshcore — P2P Chat"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh"},"2281":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-0","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","content":"**Serverless peer-to-peer chat with an automatic relay fallback.** It runs on static hosting (GitHub Pages) — zero servers, zero databases, zero accounts."},"2282":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-1","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#-meshcore--p2p-chat","content":"💬 Meshcore — P2P Chat"},"2283":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-2","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#how-it-works","content":"How it works"},"2284":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-3","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#layer-1--p2p-webrtc","content":"Layer 1 — P2P (WebRTC)"},"2285":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-4","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#layer-2--relay-automatic-fallback","content":"Layer 2 — Relay (automatic fallback)"},"2286":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-5","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#features","content":"Features"},"2287":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-6","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#how-to-use-it","content":"How to use it"},"2288":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-7","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#run-locally","content":"Run locally"},"2289":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-8","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#security","content":"Security"},"2290":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-9","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#more","content":"More"},"2291":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-10","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#-meshcore--p2p-chat","content":"<a class=\"tests-cta\" href=\"./tests\">🧪 View animated test results — 22/22 →</a>"},"2292":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-11","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#-meshcore--p2p-chat","content":"**Serverless peer-to-peer chat with an automatic relay fallback.** It runs on\nstatic hosting (GitHub Pages) — zero servers, zero databases, zero accounts."},"2293":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-12","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#-meshcore--p2p-chat","content":"> **Try it now:** 🚀 Open Meshcore\n> (opens in a new tab)"},"2294":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-13","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#layer-1--p2p-webrtc","content":"**Peers announce their presence** on a per-room MQTT topic (public broker,\nno account — the same way messengers discover each other)."},"2295":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-14","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#layer-1--p2p-webrtc","content":"When two peers see each other, they exchange **WebRTC offer/answer/ICE\ncandidates** through that topic (the classic signaling-server pattern used\nby PeerJS & co.). The broker only *introduces* peers — it never sees\nmessage payloads."},"2296":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-15","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#layer-1--p2p-webrtc","content":"Once connected, chat messages travel over the **WebRTC data channel**\ndirectly between browsers — real peer-to-peer."},"2297":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-16","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#layer-2--relay-automatic-fallback","content":"Pure browser P2P **cannot connect on mobile networks**: carriers use CGNAT\nand block NAT hole-punching, and the free public TURN servers that used to\nbridge it are dead or paid (2026). That is why every message is also\n**published in parallel** to a per-room topic on a public MQTT broker\n(WSS, no account). Every receiver **deduplicates by message ID**, so:"},"2298":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-17","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#layer-2--relay-automatic-fallback","content":"peers that can connect P2P → messages travel directly over the WebRTC data\nchannel,"},"2299":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-18","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#layer-2--relay-automatic-fallback","content":"devices that cannot (phones on LTE/5G) → exchange messages via the relay."},"2300":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-19","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#layer-2--relay-automatic-fallback","content":"**P2P first, relay as a fallback** — the same pattern real messengers use."},"2301":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-20","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#layer-2--relay-automatic-fallback","content":"> **Why not WebTorrent trackers?** The original build found peers through\n> public WebTorrent WebSocket trackers (`tracker.webtorrent.dev`,\n> `tracker.openwebtorrent.com`). Those trackers now accept announces and see\n> the swarm, but **no longer relay WebRTC offers** between peers (verified\n> live: two peers registered in the same swarm, zero offers ever returned).\n> Since the browser build of WebTorrent can only use WebSocket trackers (no\n> UDP/DHT in the browser), peers could never find each other — so signaling\n> moved to the MQTT relay."},"2302":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-21","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#features","content":"🔗 **Rooms** — the same room name = the same signaling topic = the same\npeer group"},"2303":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-22","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#features","content":"📡 **Relay fallback** — a dependency-free MQTT 3.1.1 client (\\~100 lines, no\nlibraries), publishing to `n2mesh/{room}`, room isolation by exact topic"},"2304":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-23","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#features","content":"🔀 **Deduplication by ID** — a message received over P2P *and* relay *and*\nthe local bridge is displayed once"},"2305":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-24","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#features","content":"💬 **True P2P messages** — over WebRTC data channels, with MQTT fallback"},"2306":{"id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh-25","page_id":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/projects/n2-mesh#features","content":"🏷️ Nicknames, peer counter, 